          `The biggest mistake in reading comprehension is **reading every word carefully**. Time is short in an exam. If you stop at every word, the time will run out before the passage ends. Learn to skim: focus on the important lines and let the filler words pass. In "The library opens at nine in the morning", the important words are "library", "opens", and "nine" — "at", "in", and "the" are only joining words. Stopping at them wastes time.

**Mistake 2: going back again and again.** Many students read one line three or four times because their attention wanders. This wastes time and lowers confidence. The solution is: read the questions first, then read the passage once with full attention, and underline as you go. If a line is still unclear, skip it and move on — come back to it at the end if time remains. In an exam, moving forward is better than standing still.

**Mistake 3: answering from your own knowledge.** The passage says the library opens at nine, but you feel it opens at eight because your library opens at eight. In an exam, trust only the passage — keep your own knowledge outside. Every question is asked on the basis of the passage. If the passage says "The earth is flat" (even though it is wrong), then according to the passage the answer is "flat". Treat the passage as the truth, even when it differs from what you know.`
