          `Teeno types roz ki zindagi mein dikhte hain. **News report** factual hoti hai: "The match will start at 7 pm in Mumbai. Tickets cost 500 rupees." Ismein koi opinion nahi hai, sirf facts hain. Agar koi poochhe "Match kab shuru hoga?", toh jawab seedha hai — 7 pm. News padhte waqt aap factual reading karte ho: kya, kab, kahan, kaun — yahi chaar sawaal.

**Kahani** aksar inference mangti hai. Jaise ek kahani mein likha ho: "Meera looked at the empty plate and smiled." Yahan seedha nahi likha ki Meera ko khaana pasand aaya, par inference yeh hai ki use khaana pasand aaya — kyunki khaali plate aur smile dono yahi batate hain. Kahani padhte waqt aap hamesha inference karte ho: character kya mehsoos kar raha hai, aage kya hoga — yeh sab lines ke beech se samajhna hota hai.

**Review** — jaise ek film ya restaurant ka review — tone ka best example hai. "The food was cold, the waiter was rude, and the bill was shocking" — yeh review negative tone mein hai, halanki "I hated it" kahin nahi likha. Tone words — cold, rude, shocking — se pata chal jaata hai ki reviewer naraaz hai. Review padhte waqt aap tone ko samajhkar hi decide karte ho ki wahan jaana hai ya nahi. Exam mein bhi tone wahi kaam karta hai: words se mood pakdo, phir option chuno.`,
          `All three types appear in daily life. A **news report** is factual: "The match will start at 7 pm in Mumbai. Tickets cost 500 rupees." There is no opinion in it, only facts. If someone asks "When will the match start?", the answer is direct — 7 pm. While reading news you do factual reading: what, when, where, who — those four questions.

A **story** often needs inference. For example, a story says: "Meera looked at the empty plate and smiled." It does not say directly that Meera liked the food, but the inference is that she liked it — because the empty plate and the smile both say so. While reading a story you always make inferences: what the character feels, what will happen next — all of this must be understood from between the lines.

A **review** — for example, a review of a film or a restaurant — is the best example of tone. "The food was cold, the waiter was rude, and the bill was shocking" is written in a negative tone, although "I hated it" is never written. The tone words — cold, rude, shocking — show that the reviewer is angry. While reading a review you decide whether to go there by understanding its tone. Tone works the same way in an exam: catch the mood from the words, then choose the option.`
