          `Ab ek hi passage ko teen tareeke se padhkar dry run karte hain. Passage hai: "The museum opened in 1990. It holds 200 paintings. The guide said the collection is beautiful." Pehle **factual** reading karo: museum kab khula? 1990 mein. Kitni paintings hain? 200. Yeh answers passage mein seedha likhe hain — koi soch-vichaar nahi. Factual questions mein bas keywords ko scan karo aur line ko copy kar lo.

Phir **inference** reading karo: kya passage se yeh pata chalta hai ki museum purana hai? Haan — 1990 mein khula tha, matlab 30 saal se zyada purana hai. Kya guide ko collection pasand hai? Haan — usne kaha collection beautiful hai. Yeh baatein seedha nahi likhi, par facts se nikaali ja sakti hain. Inference ka rule hai: conclusion facts par based hona chahiye, guess par nahi. Agar aap keh do "museum mein 500 paintings hain", toh woh guess hai, inference nahi — kyunki passage mein 200 likha hai.

Phir **tone** reading karo: passage ka tone kaisa hai? Words dekho: "beautiful" ek positive word hai, aur koi negative word nahi hai. Isliye tone positive ya appreciative hai. Agar passage mein likha hota "The museum is small and the paintings are dull", toh tone negative hota. Neeche wala code fact markers ginkar batata hai ki passage factual hai ya nahi — dry run mein 4 markers mile, isliye passage factual hai. Exam mein aapko code nahi, dimaag se yahi kaam karna hai: signal words dhoondho aur type pehchano.`,
          `Now let us read one passage in three ways and do a dry run. The passage is: "The museum opened in 1990. It holds 200 paintings. The guide said the collection is beautiful." First do a **factual** reading: when did the museum open? In 1990. How many paintings are there? 200. These answers are written directly in the passage — no thinking is needed. In factual questions, just scan for the keywords and copy the line.

Then do an **inference** reading: does the passage show that the museum is old? Yes — it opened in 1990, which means it is more than 30 years old. Does the guide like the collection? Yes — the guide said the collection is beautiful. These things are not written directly, but they can be drawn from the facts. The rule of inference is: the conclusion must be based on facts, not on guesses. If you say "the museum has 500 paintings", that is a guess, not an inference — because the passage says 200.

Then do a **tone** reading: what is the tone of the passage? Look at the words: "beautiful" is a positive word, and there is no negative word. So the tone is positive, or appreciative. If the passage had said "The museum is small and the paintings are dull", the tone would be negative. The code below counts fact markers to tell whether a passage is factual — in the dry run it found 4 markers, so the passage is factual. In an exam you must do this same work with your mind, not code: find the signal words and recognise the type.`
