          `**What if passage ka type hi samajh na aaye?** Koi baat nahi — type pehchanna zaroori nahi hai, question solve karna zaroori hai. Har question ko alag se dekho: kya yeh question koi fact poochh raha hai, koi conclusion, ya writer ka mood? Question ka type hi bata dega ki kya karna hai. Factual question mein line dhoondho, inference mein facts jodo, tone mein words ka mood dekho. Type sirf ek label hai; kaam question se hota hai.

**What if inference question mein do options sahi lagen?** Dono ko passage se check karo. Ek option hamesha "too far" hota hai — matlab woh conclusion facts se bahut aage badh jaata hai. Jaise passage mein likha ho "Ravi packed an umbrella" — option A kahe "It might rain", option B kahe "It will rain heavily all day and flood the city". Dono umbrella se jude hain, par B bahut aage badh gaya — passage mein flood ka koi hint nahi hai. Inference mein hamesha sabse safe aur sabse chhota conclusion chuno, jo facts ke sabse kareeb ho.

**What if tone ke options mein "neutral" bhi ho?** Neutral tone tab hota hai jab passage mein na positive words hon, na negative — sirf facts. News report ka tone aksar neutral hota hai. Agar passage mein opinion wale words dikh rahe hon — "beautiful", "terrible", "amazing" — toh tone neutral nahi hai. Neutral ko sirf tab chuno jab passage poori tarah fact-based ho aur writer ne apna mood kahin na dikhaya ho.`,
          `**What if you cannot tell the type of the passage at all?** That is fine — recognising the type is not essential; solving the question is. Look at each question separately: is this question asking for a fact, a conclusion, or the writer's mood? The type of the question itself tells you what to do. In a factual question find the line, in an inference question join the facts, and in a tone question look at the mood of the words. The type is only a label; the work comes from the question.

**What if two options look correct in an inference question?** Check both against the passage. One option is always "too far" — it goes far beyond the facts. For example, the passage says "Ravi packed an umbrella" — option A says "It might rain", and option B says "It will rain heavily all day and flood the city". Both are connected to the umbrella, but B goes far ahead — the passage gives no hint of a flood. In inference, always choose the safest and smallest conclusion, the one closest to the facts.

**What if "neutral" is also among the tone options?** A neutral tone happens when a passage has neither positive nor negative words — only facts. A news report is often neutral in tone. If the passage shows opinion words — "beautiful", "terrible", "amazing" — then the tone is not neutral. Choose neutral only when the passage is fully fact-based and the writer has shown no mood anywhere.`
