          `Teeno types ka rule ek line mein: **factual mein copy karo, inference mein socho, tone mein feel karo.** Factual passage mein dates, numbers aur naam seedha milte hain — scan karo aur jawab likh do. Inference mein facts ko jodkar woh conclusion nikaalo jo likha nahi hai, par logically nikalta hai — guess se bacho, aur "too far" wale conclusions se bhi. Tone mein poore passage ke words ka mood dekho — ek word se judge mat karo, aur sarcasm se savdhaan raho.

Mixed passage mein fact aur opinion ko alag karo: "How many?" wale questions fact se, "What does the writer think?" wale questions opinion se solve hote hain. "Can be inferred" wale question mein woh option galat hai jo passage mein seedha likha ho — inference hamesha chhupa hota hai. Neutral tone sirf tab chuno jab passage mein koi opinion word na ho.

Revision ke liye: aaj ka newspaper kholo aur ek news report ko factual, ek editorial ko tone, aur ek kahani ko inference ke hisaab se padho. Har ek mein se ek question khud banao aur jawab do. Agle article mein hum vocabulary dekhenge — roots, prefixes aur suffixes se naye words kaise banaye jaate hain.`,
          `The rule for all three types in one line: **copy for facts, think for inference, and feel for tone.** In a factual passage, dates, numbers, and names are given directly — scan and write the answer. In inference, join the facts to reach a conclusion that is not written but follows logically — avoid guesses, and avoid conclusions that go "too far". In tone, look at the mood of the words in the whole passage — do not judge from one word, and watch out for sarcasm.

In a mixed passage, separate fact from opinion: "How many?" questions are solved from facts, and "What does the writer think?" questions are solved from opinions. In a "can be inferred" question, the option written directly in the passage is wrong — an inference is always hidden. Choose a neutral tone only when the passage has no opinion word at all.

For revision: open today's newspaper and read one news report as factual, one editorial as tone, and one story as inference. Make one question from each and answer it yourself. In the next article we will look at vocabulary — how new words are built from roots, prefixes, and suffixes.`
