        body: t(
          `English ke zyada tar bade words chhote-chhote parts se bane hote hain. **Root** word ka main hissa hota hai jismein asli meaning hota hai. Jaise "port" ka matlab hai "carry karna" — is root se bante hain import, export, transport, portable. Root ka matlab jaan lo, toh poora word samajhna easy ho jaata hai. **Prefix** root ke pehle lagta hai aur meaning badal deta hai: "un-" ka matlab hai "not", isliye "happy" se "unhappy" (not happy) ban jaata hai. **Suffix** root ke baad lagta hai aur aksar word ki category badal deta hai: "help" (verb) mein "-ful" lagao toh "helpful" (adjective) ban jaata hai.

English ke bahut se roots **Latin** aur **Greek** se aaye hain. Jaise "scrib" (Latin: likhna) se bante hain describe, prescribe, subscribe, manuscript. "Dict" (Latin: bolna) se bante hain predict, dictate, dictionary. "Bio" (Greek: life) se bante hain biology, biography, biodegradable. Sirf 20-30 common roots yaad kar lo, toh exam mein aane wale 100 se zyada words ka matlab andaza lagaya ja sakta hai — bina dictionary ke.

Prefixes aur suffixes ka ek aur fayda hai: woh exam mein **word formation** wale questions mein seedha kaam aate hain. Jaise question ho "The opposite of 'agree' is ___" — "dis-" prefix lagao, jawab hai "disagree". Ya "Change 'quick' into an adverb" — "-ly" suffix lagao, jawab hai "quickly". Yeh questions ratne se nahi, pattern samajhne se solve hote hain.`,
          `Most long words in English are made of small parts. The **root** is the main part of a word, and it carries the real meaning. For example, "port" means "to carry" — from this root come import, export, transport, and portable. Once you know the meaning of the root, the whole word becomes easy to understand. A **prefix** comes before the root and changes the meaning: "un-" means "not", so "happy" becomes "unhappy" (not happy). A **suffix** comes after the root and often changes the type of the word: add "-ful" to "help" (a verb) and you get "helpful" (an adjective).

Many English roots come from **Latin** and **Greek**. For example, "scrib" (Latin: to write) gives describe, prescribe, subscribe, and manuscript. "Dict" (Latin: to speak) gives predict, dictate, and dictionary. "Bio" (Greek: life) gives biology, biography, and biodegradable. Learn just 20 to 30 common roots and you can guess the meaning of more than 100 words in an exam — without a dictionary.

Prefixes and suffixes also help directly in **word formation** questions in exams. For example, if the question is "The opposite of 'agree' is ___", add the prefix "dis-" and the answer is "disagree". Or "Change 'quick' into an adverb" — add the suffix "-ly" and the answer is "quickly". These questions are solved by understanding the pattern, not by memorising.`
        )
