        body: t(
          `**What if root ka matlab pata na ho?** Tab bhi aap andaza laga sakte ho. Pehle word ko todne ki koshish karo: kya ismein koi pehchana hua prefix ya suffix hai? Jaise "unbelievable" mein "un-" (not) aur "-able" (can be) dikh raha hai — beech mein "believe" hai. Matlab hua "not able to be believed" — jo vishwas ke layak nahi hai. Bina root jaane bhi prefix aur suffix se 70% meaning nikal aata hai. Aur agar kuch bhi na pehchano, toh sentence ka context dekho — sentence mein word kaise use hua hai, usse meaning andaza lagao.

**What if word root se bana hi na ho?** Kai chhote aur roz ke words — jaise "cat", "run", "book" — kisi root se nahi bane. Inhe root method se samajhne ki koshish mat karo; inhe waise hi yaad rakho. Root method bade aur mushkil words ke liye hai — jaise "transportation", "biodegradable", "circumference". Chhote words ko todne mein time barbaad mat karo.

**What if do roots ek jaise lagen?** Jaise "port" (carry) aur "port" (bandargah) — ek hi spelling, do meanings. "Portable" mein "port" ka matlab carry hai, "airport" mein port ka matlab bandargah (hawai adda) hai. Context se decide karo: agar word kisi cheez ko le jaane ki baat kar raha hai, toh root "carry" hai; agar jagah ki baat kar raha hai, toh "port" ka matlab bandargah hai. Exam mein aise confusing words mein sentence ko dhyaan se padho — sentence hi batayega ki kaunsa meaning sahi hai.`,
          `**What if you do not know the meaning of the root?** You can still guess. First try to break the word apart: does it have a prefix or suffix you recognise? In "unbelievable" you can see "un-" (not) and "-able" (can be), with "believe" in the middle. The meaning becomes "not able to be believed" — something that cannot be believed. Even without knowing the root, the prefix and suffix give about 70% of the meaning. And if you recognise nothing at all, look at the context of the sentence — how the word is used in the sentence will suggest its meaning.

**What if the word is not built from a root at all?** Many short, everyday words — such as "cat", "run", and "book" — are not built from any root. Do not try to understand them with the root method; learn them as they are. The root method is for long and difficult words — such as "transportation", "biodegradable", and "circumference". Do not waste time breaking short words apart.

**What if two roots look alike?** For example, "port" (to carry) and "port" (a harbour) — the same spelling, two meanings. In "portable", "port" means to carry; in "airport", "port" means a harbour (an airport). Decide from the context: if the word talks about carrying something, the root means "carry"; if it talks about a place, "port" means a harbour. In an exam, read the sentence carefully for such confusing words — the sentence itself will show which meaning is correct.`
        )
