          `Kuch words ke **kai synonyms** hote hain, aur exam mein sirf ek hi option mein hota hai. Jaise "beautiful" ke synonyms hain pretty, lovely, gorgeous, attractive. Options mein sirf ek hoga — wahi jawab hai. Par kabhi do synonyms options mein aa jaate hain — jaise "pretty" aur "lovely" dono. Tab woh chuno jo word ke sabse kareeb ho: "beautiful" ka sabse kareeb hai "lovely" ya "gorgeous", "pretty" thoda halka hai. Aise mein nuance (bareek farq) decide karta hai. Nuance samajhne ke liye words ko sentences mein use karke dekho: "a beautiful sunset" sahi hai, "a pretty sunset" bhi sahi hai, par "a gorgeous sunset" zyada strong hai.

**Graded antonyms** doosra edge case hai. Kuch antonyms absolute hote hain — jaise "alive" aur "dead": koi beech ki stage nahi hai. Par kuch antonyms graded hote hain — jaise "hot" aur "cold": beech mein "warm" aur "cool" bhi hain. Exam mein jab "hot" ka antonym poochha jaaye, toh "cold" hi jawab hai, "cool" nahi — kyunki "cold" sabse opposite hai. Rule hai: antonym mein sabse door wala word chuno, beech wala nahi.

**Teesra edge case: same word, alag antonym.** "Right" ka antonym "wrong" bhi hai aur "left" bhi hai — context par depend karta hai. "The right answer" mein antonym hai "wrong answer"; "turn right" mein antonym hai "turn left". Exam mein hamesha sentence dekho — sentence hi batayega ki kaunsa antonym sahi hai. Bina context ke antonym chunna galat ho sakta hai.`,
          `Some words have **many synonyms**, and only one of them appears in the exam options. For example, the synonyms of "beautiful" are pretty, lovely, gorgeous, and attractive. Only one will be in the options — that is the answer. But sometimes two synonyms appear in the options — for example, "pretty" and "lovely" both. Then choose the one closest to the word: the closest to "beautiful" is "lovely" or "gorgeous"; "pretty" is a little lighter. In such cases the nuance (the fine difference) decides. To understand nuance, try the words in sentences: "a beautiful sunset" is correct, "a pretty sunset" is also correct, but "a gorgeous sunset" is stronger.

**Graded antonyms** are a second edge case. Some antonyms are absolute — such as "alive" and "dead": there is no stage in between. But some antonyms are graded — such as "hot" and "cold": in between there are also "warm" and "cool". In an exam, when the antonym of "hot" is asked, the answer is "cold", not "cool" — because "cold" is the most opposite. The rule is: in an antonym, choose the word that is farthest away, not one in the middle.

A **third edge case: the same word, a different antonym.** The antonym of "right" is "wrong" and also "left" — it depends on the context. In "the right answer", the antonym is "wrong answer"; in "turn right", the antonym is "turn left". In an exam, always look at the sentence — the sentence itself will show which antonym is correct. Choosing an antonym without context can be wrong.`
