          `Ab hum do definitions ka step-by-step dry run karte hain. Definition 1: "a person who writes books". Step 1: definition mein key word dhoondho — "writes books". Step 2: socho, books likhne wale ko kya kehte hain? Author. Step 3: options se match karo — agar options hon (a) author (b) reader (c) publisher (d) librarian, toh "author" sahi hai. "Reader" books padhta hai, "publisher" books chhapta hai, "librarian" library sambhalta hai — sirf "author" books likhta hai. Dry run complete: definition ka har word option se match kiya gaya.

Definition 2: "a place where birds are kept". Step 1: key words hain "place" aur "birds". Step 2: birds rakhne ki jagah ko kya kehte hain? Aviary. Step 3: options check karo — (a) apiary (b) aviary (c) mortuary (d) library. "Apiary" mein bees rakhi jaati hain (api = bee), "mortuary" mein dead bodies rakhi jaati hain, "library" mein books rakhi jaati hain. Sirf "aviary" mein birds rakhe jaate hain — "avi" ka matlab hai bird. Yahan root "avi" (bird) ne jawab de diya.

Neeche wala code yahi mapping karta hai: ek dictionary mein definition ko word se joda gaya hai, aur phir har definition ke liye uska word print kiya jaata hai. Dry run: pehli line mein "a person who writes books -> author" print hota hai, doosri mein "a place where birds are kept -> aviary". Code mein dictionary ka use dekho — \`words[phrase]\` se definition ka word milta hai. Exam mein aap yahi kaam dimaag mein karte ho: definition ko yaad kiye hue word se match karo.`,
          `Now let us do a step-by-step dry run of two definitions. Definition 1: "a person who writes books". Step 1: find the key words in the definition — "writes books". Step 2: think, what is a person who writes books called? An author. Step 3: match it with the options — if the options are (a) author (b) reader (c) publisher (d) librarian, then "author" is correct. A "reader" reads books, a "publisher" prints books, and a "librarian" looks after a library — only an "author" writes books. Dry run complete: every word of the definition was matched with an option.

Definition 2: "a place where birds are kept". Step 1: the key words are "place" and "birds". Step 2: what is a place where birds are kept called? An aviary. Step 3: check the options — (a) apiary (b) aviary (c) mortuary (d) library. In an "apiary", bees are kept (api = bee); in a "mortuary", dead bodies are kept; in a "library", books are kept. Only in an "aviary" are birds kept — "avi" means bird. Here the root "avi" (bird) gave the answer.

The code below does this same mapping: a dictionary joins each definition to its word, and then the word for every definition is printed. Here is the dry run: the first line prints "a person who writes books -> author", and the second prints "a place where birds are kept -> aviary". Look at how the dictionary is used in the code — \`words[phrase]\` gives the word for a definition. In an exam you do this same work in your mind: match the definition with a word you have learned.`
