          `Kabhi ek definition ke **do words** hote hain — jaise "a person who loves books" ke liye "bibliophile" bhi hai aur "bookworm" bhi hai. Dono ka matlab lagbhag same hai, par "bibliophile" formal hai aur "bookworm" informal hai. Exam mein formal word hi jawab hota hai, kyunki exam formal vocabulary test karta hai. Agar dono options mein hon, toh formal wala chuno. "Bookworm" roz ki bol-chaal mein sahi hai, par exam mein "bibliophile" hi aayega.

**Negative definitions** doosra edge case hai — jaise "one who does not believe in God" (atheist), "one who eats only vegetables" (vegetarian). Negative definitions mein "not" ya "only" par dhyaan do — yahi words jawab decide karte hain. "One who believes in God" ka jawab hai "theist", "one who does not believe in God" ka jawab hai "atheist" — sirf "a-" prefix ka farq hai. Definition mein "not" ko miss karna matlab galat jawab chunna.

**Teesra edge case: "one who" vs "a place where".** Definition ke pehle words hi bata dete hain ki jawab kis category ka hoga. "One who..." se shuru ho toh jawab ek person hoga (author, miser, optimist). "A place where..." se shuru ho toh jawab ek jagah hogi (aviary, apiary, mortuary). "The study of..." se shuru ho toh jawab ek subject hoga (biology, geology). Pehle do words padhte hi aapko pata chal jaata hai ki kis type ka word dhoondhna hai — isse options ko filter karna easy ho jaata hai.`,
          `Sometimes one definition has **two words** — for example, for "a person who loves books" there is both "bibliophile" and "bookworm". Both mean almost the same thing, but "bibliophile" is formal and "bookworm" is informal. In an exam the formal word is the answer, because exams test formal vocabulary. If both are in the options, choose the formal one. "Bookworm" is fine in daily speech, but in an exam only "bibliophile" will appear.

**Negative definitions** are a second edge case — for example, "one who does not believe in God" (atheist), or "one who eats only vegetables" (vegetarian). In negative definitions, pay attention to "not" or "only" — these words decide the answer. The answer to "one who believes in God" is "theist", and to "one who does not believe in God" it is "atheist" — only the prefix "a-" differs. Missing "not" in the definition means choosing the wrong answer.

A **third edge case: "one who" versus "a place where".** The first words of the definition already tell you which category the answer belongs to. If it starts with "One who...", the answer is a person (author, miser, optimist). If it starts with "A place where...", the answer is a place (aviary, apiary, mortuary). If it starts with "The study of...", the answer is a subject (biology, geology). As soon as you read the first two words, you know what type of word to look for — this makes it easy to filter the options.`
