          `**"At night"** ek famous exception hai. Raat bada period hai, isliye rule ke hisaab se "in night" hona chahiye, par sahi hai "at night". Isi tarah "at noon" aur "at midnight" bhi exceptions hain. Inhe alag se yaad karo: at night, at noon, at midnight, at dawn. Par "in the morning", "in the afternoon", "in the evening" mein "in" hi aata hai. Sirf raat aur uske aas-paas ke time mein "at" aata hai — yeh exception yaad rakhna zaroori hai.

**"In the end" vs "at the end"** doosra edge case hai. "In the end" ka matlab hai aakhir mein (finally): "In the end, we won the match." "At the end" ka matlab hai kisi cheez ke aakhri hisse mein: "At the end of the book, there is a summary." Farq hai: "in the end" mein "of" nahi aata, "at the end" mein aksar "of" aata hai (at the end of the day, at the end of the road). Exam mein yeh farq aksar poochha jaata hai.

**"On time" vs "in time"** teesra edge case hai. "On time" ka matlab hai exact time par: "The train arrived on time" (train 6 baje aani thi aur 6 baje hi aayi). "In time" ka matlab hai time se pehle, der hone se pehle: "We reached in time for the movie" (movie shuru hone se pehle pahunch gaye). "On time" = punctual, "in time" = before it is too late. Dono mein "time" hai, par meaning alag hai — yeh farq yaad rakho.`,
          `**"At night"** is a famous exception. Night is a large period, so by the rule it should be "in night", but the correct form is "at night". In the same way, "at noon" and "at midnight" are also exceptions. Learn them separately: at night, at noon, at midnight, at dawn. But "in the morning", "in the afternoon", and "in the evening" still take "in". Only night and the times around it take "at" — remembering this exception is essential.

**"In the end" versus "at the end"** is a second edge case. "In the end" means finally: "In the end, we won the match." "At the end" means in the last part of something: "At the end of the book, there is a summary." The difference is: "in the end" does not take "of", while "at the end" often takes "of" (at the end of the day, at the end of the road). Exams often ask about this difference.

**"On time" versus "in time"** is a third edge case. "On time" means at the exact time: "The train arrived on time" (the train was due at 6 and came at 6). "In time" means before it is too late: "We reached in time for the movie" (we arrived before the movie started). "On time" = punctual; "in time" = before it is too late. Both have "time", but the meanings differ — remember this difference.`
