          `Tense mein sabse zyada galtiyan chaar jagah par hoti hain. **Galti 1: \`since\` aur \`for\` ka confusion.** \`Since\` ke saath ek point of time aata hai — \`since 2015\`, \`since morning\`, \`since Monday\`. \`For\` ke saath ek duration aata hai — \`for five years\`, \`for two hours\`, \`for a long time\`. "I have lived here **for** 2015" galat hai; sahi hai "I have lived here **since** 2015" ya "I have lived here **for** ten years."

**Galti 2: \`has\` aur \`have\` ka galat use.** \`He\`, \`she\` aur \`it\` ke saath \`has\` aata hai; \`I\`, \`you\`, \`we\` aur \`they\` ke saath \`have\` aata hai. "She **have** finished" galat hai; sahi hai "She **has** finished." Isi tarah simple present mein \`he\` ke saath verb mein **-s** lagta hai: "He **play**" galat hai, "He **plays**" sahi hai.

**Galti 3: Simple past ke saath \`since\` lagana.** "I **lived** here since 2015" galat hai, kyunki \`since\` ka matlab hai kaam abhi bhi chal raha hai. Sahi hai "I **have lived** here since 2015." Yaad rakho: \`since\` + present perfect, kabhi simple past nahi.

**Galti 4: Future mein \`will\` ke baad verb ka galat form.** \`Will\` ke baad hamesha verb ka base form aata hai: "She **will plays**" galat hai, "She **will play**" sahi hai. Isi tarah \`did\` ke baad bhi base form aata hai: "She **did played**" galat hai, "She **did play**" sahi hai. Modal aur helping verb ke baad main verb kabhi **-s** ya **-ed** nahi leta.`,
          `Tense mistakes happen most often in four places. **Mistake 1: confusing \`since\` and \`for\`.** \`Since\` goes with a point in time — \`since 2015\`, \`since morning\`, \`since Monday\`. \`For\` goes with a length of time — \`for five years\`, \`for two hours\`, \`for a long time\`. "I have lived here **for** 2015" is wrong; the correct forms are "I have lived here **since** 2015" or "I have lived here **for** ten years."

**Mistake 2: using \`has\` and \`have\` wrongly.** \`He\`, \`she\`, and \`it\` take \`has\`; \`I\`, \`you\`, \`we\`, and \`they\` take \`have\`. "She **have** finished" is wrong; the correct form is "She **has** finished." In the same way, in the simple present \`he\` needs **-s** on the verb: "He **play**" is wrong, "He **plays**" is right.

**Mistake 3: using \`since\` with the simple past.** "I **lived** here since 2015" is wrong, because \`since\` means the action is still going on. The correct form is "I **have lived** here since 2015." Remember: \`since\` goes with the present perfect, never with the simple past.

**Mistake 4: using the wrong verb form after \`will\`.** After \`will\`, the verb always takes its base form: "She **will plays**" is wrong, "She **will play**" is right. In the same way, after \`did\` the base form is used: "She **did played**" is wrong, "She **did play**" is right. After a modal or a helping verb, the main verb never takes **-s** or **-ed**.`
