          `Examiner ko sabse zyada pasand hai do tenses ke beech ka farak poochhna. **What if: present perfect ya simple past?** Dono past ki baat karte hain, par farak hai: present perfect ka asar **abhi** hai, simple past ka asar **khatam** ho chuka hai. "I **have lost** my key" matlab key abhi bhi khoi hui hai (asar abhi hai). "I **lost** my key yesterday" matlab kal khoyi thi, aur shayad mil bhi gayi ho. Agar sentence mein \`yesterday\`, \`last week\` ya \`ago\` ho, toh simple past pakka hai. Agar \`just\`, \`already\`, \`yet\` ya \`ever\` ho, toh present perfect pakka hai.

**What if: continuous ya simple?** Continuous ka matlab hai kaam **abhi chal raha** hai aur temporary hai; simple ka matlab hai kaam **aadat** hai ya permanent hai. "I **am living** in Delhi these days" matlab abhi temporary Delhi mein ho. "I **live** in Delhi" matlab Delhi tumhara permanent ghar hai. Exam mein \`these days\` ya \`at the moment\` dikhe toh continuous chuno; \`always\` ya \`every day\` dikhe toh simple chuno.

**What if: future ke liye \`will\` ya \`going to\`?** Dono future batate hain, par \`going to\` tab use hota hai jab plan pehle se bana ho ya saboot dikh raha ho: "Look at the clouds, it **is going to rain**." \`Will\` tab use hota hai jab decision abhi liya ho: "I **will help** you." Dono galat nahi hote, par context ke hisaab se ek zyada sahi hota hai — exam mein plan ya saboot dikhe toh \`going to\` chuno.

**What if: past perfect kab chahiye?** Past perfect tab chahiye jab past mein **do kaam** hon aur ek doosre se pehle hua ho. "When I reached the station, the train **had left**" — train ka jaana pehle hua, mera pahunchna baad mein. Agar past mein sirf ek kaam hai, toh simple past hi kaafi hai: "I **reached** the station yesterday."`,
          `Examiners love to ask about the difference between two tenses. **What if: present perfect or simple past?** Both talk about the past, but the difference is this: the present perfect still has an effect **now**, while the simple past is **finished**. "I **have lost** my key" means the key is still lost (the effect is present now). "I **lost** my key yesterday" means it was lost yesterday, and it may have been found since. If the sentence has \`yesterday\`, \`last week\`, or \`ago\`, the simple past is certain. If it has \`just\`, \`already\`, \`yet\`, or \`ever\`, the present perfect is certain.

**What if: continuous or simple?** The continuous means the action is **in progress now** and temporary; the simple means the action is a **habit** or permanent. "I **am living** in Delhi these days" means you are in Delhi temporarily right now. "I **live** in Delhi" means Delhi is your permanent home. In an exam, if you see \`these days\` or \`at the moment\`, choose the continuous; if you see \`always\` or \`every day\`, choose the simple.

**What if: \`will\` or \`going to\` for the future?** Both show the future, but \`going to\` is used when a plan was already made or the evidence is visible: "Look at the clouds, it **is going to rain**." \`Will\` is used when the decision is made just now: "I **will help** you." Neither is wrong, but one fits the context better — in an exam, if there is a plan or visible evidence, choose \`going to\`.

**What if: when is the past perfect needed?** The past perfect is needed when there are **two actions** in the past and one happened before the other. "When I reached the station, the train **had left**" — the train left first, and my arrival came later. If there is only one action in the past, the simple past is enough: "I **reached** the station yesterday."`
