          `Narration means **reporting** what someone said. In direct speech we write the exact words inside quotation marks: Ravi said, **"I am happy."** Here the words are exactly Ravi's, with nothing changed. In indirect speech we report the same words in our own words: Ravi said **that he was happy.** Here there are no quotation marks, \`that\` has been added, \`I\` has changed to \`he\`, and \`am\` has changed to \`was\`.

Direct speech has two parts: the **reporting verb** (\`said\`, \`told\`, \`asked\`) and the **reported speech** (the words inside the quotation marks). The reporting verb tells you who spoke, and the reported speech tells you what was said. In indirect speech, the reported speech joins the reporting verb with \`that\` (for a statement), \`if/whether\` (for a yes-no question), or a \`wh-word\` (for a wh-question).

Three things change in narration: the **tense** (present changes to past), the **pronoun** (\`I\` changes to \`he/she\`), and the **time words** (\`today\` changes to \`that day\`). But one thing never changes: the **meaning**. Direct and indirect speech always mean the same thing. Exams ask narration questions because they test tense, pronoun, and time all at once.`
