          `In some cases the tense does not change. **Universal truth:** "The teacher said that the sun **rises** in the east" — here \`rises\` stays in the present, it does not become \`rose\`, because the sun always rises in the east. A habitual fact also does not change: "She said that she **goes** to school every day" — it is a daily habit, so it stays in the present.

**Reporting verb in the present:** "Ravi **says** that he is happy" — here the reporting verb is in the present, so the tense of the reported speech does not change. In the same way, "Ravi **has said** that he is happy" does not change the tense either, because the reporting verb is in the present perfect. Tense shift happens only when the reporting verb is in the **simple past**.

**Order and request:** an order takes \`to + verb\`. The indirect form of "The boss said, 'Finish the work'" is "The boss **ordered to finish** the work" — or more naturally, "The boss **ordered him to finish** the work." A request takes \`requested\`: the indirect form of "She said, 'Please help me'" is "She **requested him to help** her." An order takes \`ordered\`, a request takes \`requested\`, and advice takes \`advised\` — the reporting verb changes according to the meaning of the sentence.

**Exclamation:** the indirect form of "She said, 'What a beautiful day!'" is "She **exclaimed** that it was a beautiful day." In an exclamation the reporting verb is \`exclaimed\` and \`that\` is used. In a \`what\` or \`how\` exclamation, those words are dropped in the indirect form and the sentence takes statement order.`
