          `Agreement mein sabse zyada galtiyan chaar jagah par hoti hain. **Galti 1: paas wale noun se verb match karna.** "The box of chocolates **are** on the table" galat hai — verb \`box\` se match karega, \`chocolates\` se nahi. \`Of\`, \`with\`, \`along with\` aur \`as well as\` ke baad wala noun kabhi subject nahi hota. "Ravi, along with his friends, **is** coming" — yahan subject sirf \`Ravi\` hai, friends sirf saath mein hain, isliye verb singular \`is\` aayega.

**Galti 2: \`each\` aur \`every\` ke baad plural verb lagana.** "Each student **have** a book" galat hai; sahi hai "Each student **has** a book." "Every boy and girl **are** present" galat hai; sahi hai "Every boy and girl **is** present." \`Each\` aur \`every\` hamesha singular hote hain, chahe sentence mein do nouns hi kyun na hon.

**Galti 3: collective nouns ko hamesha plural samajhna.** \`Team\`, \`family\`, \`crowd\` aur \`committee\` — ye ek group ko batate hain, isliye aam taur par singular verb lete hain: "The team **is** playing well", "My family **is** large." Lekin jab group ke members alag-alag kaam kar rahe hon, toh plural bhi ho sakta hai: "The team **are** arguing among themselves." Exam mein aam taur par singular hi sahi mana jaata hai.

**Galti 4: \`news\`, \`mathematics\` aur \`physics\` ko plural samajhna.** Ye words end mein **-s** se khatam hote hain, par singular hain: "The news **is** good", "Mathematics **is** my favourite subject", "Physics **is** tough." Isi tarah \`scissors\`, \`trousers\` aur \`glasses\` plural hain: "The scissors **are** sharp", "My trousers **are** new."`,
          `Agreement mistakes happen most often in four places. **Mistake 1: matching the verb with the nearest noun.** "The box of chocolates **are** on the table" is wrong — the verb must agree with \`box\`, not with \`chocolates\`. The noun after \`of\`, \`with\`, \`along with\`, or \`as well as\` is never the subject. "Ravi, along with his friends, **is** coming" — here the subject is only \`Ravi\`, the friends are only with him, so the verb is the singular \`is\`.

**Mistake 2: using a plural verb after \`each\` and \`every\`.** "Each student **have** a book" is wrong; the correct form is "Each student **has** a book." "Every boy and girl **are** present" is wrong; the correct form is "Every boy and girl **is** present." \`Each\` and \`every\` are always singular, even when the sentence has two nouns.

**Mistake 3: always treating collective nouns as plural.** \`Team\`, \`family\`, \`crowd\`, and \`committee\` name one group, so they usually take a singular verb: "The team **is** playing well", "My family **is** large." But when the members of the group act separately, the plural is also possible: "The team **are** arguing among themselves." In exams, the singular is usually treated as correct.

**Mistake 4: treating \`news\`, \`mathematics\`, and \`physics\` as plural.** These words end in **-s**, but they are singular: "The news **is** good", "Mathematics **is** my favourite subject", "Physics **is** tough." In the same way, \`scissors\`, \`trousers\`, and \`glasses\` are plural: "The scissors **are** sharp", "My trousers **are** new."`
