          `Kuch cases mein rule thoda alag hota hai. **Either/or aur neither/nor:** paas wala subject decide karta hai. "Either Ravi or his friends **are** coming" — \`friends\` plural hai, isliye \`are\`. "Either his friends or Ravi **is** coming" — \`Ravi\` singular hai, isliye \`is\`. Exam mein aksar do options mein sirf order badal kar confuse kiya jaata hai.

**None:** "None of the students **is** present" aur "None of the students **are** present" — dono chalte hain, par formal exam mein singular \`is\` zyada safe hai. \`None\` ka matlab hai "not one", isliye singular logic se sahi hai. Lekin modern English mein plural bhi accept kiya jaata hai, isliye exam mein agar dono options hon, toh singular chuno.

**Percentage aur fraction:** verb us noun se match karta hai jiske baare mein percentage hai. "Fifty percent of the cake **is** eaten" — cake singular hai, isliye \`is\`. "Fifty percent of the students **are** present" — students plural hain, isliye \`are\`. Percentage khud kabhi subject nahi hota, uske baad wala noun subject hota hai.

**There + verb:** "There **is** a book on the table" — yahan asli subject \`a book\` hai, jo verb ke baad aata hai. "There **are** five books on the table" — yahan \`books\` plural hai, isliye \`are\`. \`There\` se shuru hone wale sentence mein verb ke baad wala noun dekho, pehle wala nahi.`,
          `In some cases the rule works a little differently. **Either/or and neither/nor:** the nearer subject decides. "Either Ravi or his friends **are** coming" — \`friends\` is plural, so \`are\` is used. "Either his friends or Ravi **is** coming" — \`Ravi\` is singular, so \`is\` is used. Exams often confuse you by changing only the order in the two options.

**None:** "None of the students **is** present" and "None of the students **are** present" — both work, but in a formal exam the singular \`is\` is safer. \`None\` means "not one", so the singular is correct by logic. But modern English also accepts the plural, so if both options appear in an exam, choose the singular.

**Percentage and fraction:** the verb agrees with the noun the percentage is about. "Fifty percent of the cake **is** eaten" — the cake is singular, so \`is\` is used. "Fifty percent of the students **are** present" — the students are plural, so \`are\` is used. The percentage itself is never the subject; the noun after it is the subject.

**There + verb:** "There **is** a book on the table" — here the real subject is \`a book\`, which comes after the verb. "There **are** five books on the table" — here \`books\` is plural, so \`are\` is used. In a sentence that starts with \`there\`, look at the noun after the verb, not the one before it.`
