          `The whole article in one line: **the verb always agrees with the real subject — a singular subject takes a singular verb, a plural subject takes a plural verb — and the phrase in the middle, the nearest noun, or the part with \`with\` never changes the subject.** Finding the real subject is the biggest skill in agreement. Remove the middle phrase, look at the number of the subject, then choose the verb.

Remember these key points: **-s** on a verb means a singular verb; \`each\`, \`every\`, \`neither\`, \`either\`, and \`one\` are always singular; subjects joined by \`and\` are usually plural, but \`with\` and \`as well as\` keep the first subject; in \`either/or\` and \`neither/nor\` the nearer subject wins; \`news\`, \`mathematics\`, and \`physics\` are singular; in a sentence with \`there\`, look at the noun after the verb.

For revision, do one thing: write 10 sentences yourself, and in each one put a long phrase between the subject and the verb — like "The box of chocolates", "The quality of the mangoes", "Ravi, along with his friends" — and then fill in the correct verb. If you get 10 out of 10 right, you know agreement. Finally, read the 5 key notes out loud once. In the next article we will look at modals and conditionals — where \`can\`, \`could\`, \`should\`, and \`would\` show possibility and condition.`
