          `Cloze test mistakes happen most often in four places. **Mistake 1: looking only at grammar, not at meaning.** In "Ravi was very ___ after the race", both \`tired\` and \`happy\` are correct by grammar, but by meaning only \`tired\` is correct, because a race brings tiredness. Grammar can make two options look correct, but meaning makes only one correct. Check both.

**Mistake 2: not reading the whole passage.** A student who gets stuck on the first blank misses the hints that come later. The end of the passage often hides the answer to the first blank. Read the whole passage first, then fill the blanks. It is also fine to fill the first blank at the end, if its hint comes later.

**Mistake 3: ignoring the tone.** If the passage is positive, a negative word can never be the answer. In "Reading increases our knowledge", \`decreases\` can never appear, even if it fits by grammar. First decide the tone of the passage — positive, negative, or neutral — then filter the options by tone. Tone at once removes half the options.

**Mistake 4: ignoring collocation.** Some words always come together: \`make a decision\`, \`take a break\`, \`pay attention\`, \`heavy rain\`. "Take a decision" is wrong, "make a decision" is correct. "Strong rain" is wrong, "heavy rain" is correct. Cloze tests have many collocation blanks, so remember common collocations.`
