          `In a cloze test, some situations need a change of strategy. **What if: you do not understand the topic of the passage?** Then fill the easy blanks first — preposition, article, and verb form blanks, because they need only grammar, not meaning. After filling the easy blanks, the meaning of the passage starts to become clear on its own, and then you can fill the hard blanks.

**What if: time is short?** Then read the passage once quickly and for each blank first remove the option that is clearly wrong. If two of the four options are removed, the chance becomes 50%. Then choose one of the remaining two by tone and grammar. When time is short, do not spend too long on one blank — move ahead and come back at the end.

**What if: one blank is left over?** Then do not leave it empty — make your best guess. Cloze tests usually have no negative marking, so guessing is better than leaving a blank empty. When you guess, keep both tone and grammar in mind: a positive word in a positive passage, a base form after \`to\`.

**What if: one word keeps repeating in the passage?** Then that word is the keyword of the topic. For example, in a reading passage, \`knowledge\`, \`books\`, and \`learn\` will repeat. Once you identify the keyword, the answers to the blanks will come from the same field. The keyword fixes the topic of the passage.`
