        body: t(
          `Critical reasoning mein aapko ek chhota sa argument padhna hota hai — koi keh raha hai ki kuch hona chahiye ya kuch sach hai. Argument ke teen parts hote hain. **Premise** woh facts hain jo diye gaye hain, jaise "Sales pichhle saal 20% badhi". **Conclusion** woh claim hai jo premise se nikala gaya hai, jaise "Is saal bhi sales badhegi". **Assumption** woh chhupi hui baat hai jo likhi nahi hoti par conclusion ke liye zaroori hoti hai, jaise "Market ki halat waisi hi rahegi".

Exam mein aksar yeh poochha jaata hai: conclusion kya hai, assumption kya hai, kaun sa option argument ko **strengthen** karta hai, kaun sa **weaken** karta hai, ya argument mein kya **flaw** hai. Har sawaal ka jawab argument ke parts ko alag karke milta hai. Agar aap premise aur conclusion ko mix kar doge, toh assumption kabhi sahi nahi milegi.

Ek simple rule yaad rakho: conclusion aksar words jaise **therefore, so, hence, thus, should** ke paas hota hai. Premise aksar **because, since, as** ke baad aata hai. Assumption kabhi seedha nahi likha hota — woh woh baat hoti hai jiske bina conclusion gir jaaye. Agar assumption ko ulta kar do aur conclusion toot jaaye, toh wahi sahi assumption hai.`,
          `In critical reasoning, you read a short argument — someone claims that something should happen or that something is true. An argument has three parts. A **premise** is a fact that is given, such as "Sales grew by 20% last year". A **conclusion** is the claim drawn from the premise, such as "Sales will grow this year too". An **assumption** is the hidden point that is never written down but is needed for the conclusion, such as "Market conditions will stay the same".

Exams often ask: what is the conclusion, what is the assumption, which option **strengthens** the argument, which one **weakens** it, or what is the **flaw** in the argument. Every answer comes from separating the parts of the argument. If you mix up the premise and the conclusion, you will never find the right assumption.

Remember one simple rule: the conclusion often sits near words like **therefore, so, hence, thus, should**. The premise often follows **because, since, as**. The assumption is never written directly — it is the point without which the conclusion falls apart. If you reverse the assumption and the conclusion breaks, that is the correct assumption.`
        )
