          `Confusables mein chaar galtiyan baar-baar hoti hain. **Galti 1: affect ko noun ki tarah use karna.** Log likhte hain "The affect of the rain was bad" — yeh galat hai, kyunki yahan result ki baat ho rahi hai, isliye **effect** chahiye. Yaad rakho: "the" ke baad hamesha **effect** aata hai (the effect, an effect). **Galti 2: effect ko verb ki tarah use karna.** "The rain will effect the match" galat hai — yahan verb chahiye, isliye **affect** likho. Bas ek exception hai: "to effect a change" ka matlab hota hai "badlaav laana", par yeh formal English hai aur exam mein kam aata hai.

**Galti 3: lose ki jagah loose likhna.** "I will loose my keys" galat hai — loose ka matlab dheela hota hai, kho dena nahi. Sahi hai "I will **lose** my keys". Loose mein double "o" hai, jaise "goose" mein — aur goose dheela-dhaala pakshi hota hai, yeh yaad rakhne ka mazedaar tareeka hai. **Galti 4: principal aur principle ko ulta likhna.** "Honesty is a good principal" galat hai — yahan rule ki baat ho rahi hai, isliye **principle** chahiye. Principal mein "pal" chhupa hai — school ka principal aapka pal (friend) hota hai, isliye principal ka matlab school head hai.`,
          `Four mistakes keep repeating with confusables. **Mistake 1: using affect as a noun.** People write "The affect of the rain was bad" — this is wrong, because the result is being talked about, so **effect** is needed. Remember: after "the" you always get **effect** (the effect, an effect). **Mistake 2: using effect as a verb.** "The rain will effect the match" is wrong — a verb is needed here, so write **affect**. There is just one exception: "to effect a change" means "to bring about a change", but that is formal English and rarely appears in exams.

**Mistake 3: writing loose instead of lose.** "I will loose my keys" is wrong — loose means not tight, not to misplace something. The correct form is "I will **lose** my keys". Loose has a double "o", like "goose" — and a goose is a loose, waddling bird, which is a fun way to remember it. **Mistake 4: swapping principal and principle.** "Honesty is a good principal" is wrong — a rule is being talked about here, so **principle** is needed. Principal hides the word "pal" — the school principal is your pal (friend), so principal means the head of a school.`
