          `Punctuation roz ki zindagi mein har jagah hai. Maan lo aap shopping list likhte ho: "I need apples, bananas, milk and bread." Yahan comma se cheezein alag-alag dikhti hain. Agar aap comma na lagao — "I need apples bananas milk and bread" — toh list padhna mushkil ho jaata hai. Aakhri item se pehle "and" ke baad comma lagana ya na lagana aapki pasand hai (Oxford comma), par list mein comma lagana zaroori hai.

Doosra example hai **apostrophe**. "The dogs tail is wagging" galat hai — sahi hai "The **dog's** tail is wagging", kyunki tail dog ki hai. Aur "Its raining" galat hai — sahi hai "**It's** raining", kyunki it's ka matlab hai it is. Email mein bhi punctuation kaam aata hai: "Dear Sir, I am writing to apply for the job" mein comma ke baad sentence shuru hota hai, aur letter ke end mein full stop lagta hai. Interview mein jab aap apna resume likhte ho, toh har point ke end mein full stop lagana zaroori hai — bina full stop ke resume adhura lagta hai.

Teesra example hai **colon aur semicolon**. "I need three things: a pen, a book and a bag" mein colon list se pehle aata hai. "I like tea; my brother likes coffee" mein semicolon do related sentences ko jodta hai — dono sentences ka matlab poora hai, par woh ek doosre se jude hain. Agar aap semicolon ki jagah comma lagao — "I like tea, my brother likes coffee" — toh yeh **comma splice** ban jaata hai, jo ek badi galti hai.`,
          `Punctuation is everywhere in daily life. Suppose you write a shopping list: "I need apples, bananas, milk and bread." Here the commas keep the items separate. If you leave out the commas — "I need apples bananas milk and bread" — the list becomes hard to read. Whether you put a comma before the final "and" (the Oxford comma) is your choice, but commas inside a list are necessary.

A second example is the **apostrophe**. "The dogs tail is wagging" is wrong — the correct form is "The **dog's** tail is wagging", because the tail belongs to the dog. And "Its raining" is wrong — the correct form is "**It's** raining", because it's means it is. Punctuation also matters in an email: in "Dear Sir, I am writing to apply for the job", a sentence starts after the comma, and a full stop ends the letter. When you write your resume for an interview, a full stop at the end of every point is necessary — without full stops, a resume looks unfinished.

A third example is the **colon and semicolon**. In "I need three things: a pen, a book and a bag", the colon comes before a list. In "I like tea; my brother likes coffee", the semicolon joins two related sentences — both sentences are complete on their own, but they are connected to each other. If you put a comma instead of a semicolon — "I like tea, my brother likes coffee" — that becomes a **comma splice**, which is a big mistake.`
