        body: t(
          `**Parallelism** ka matlab hai ki jab ek sentence mein do ya zyada cheezein ek hi list mein aayein, toh un sab ki **form ek jaisi** honi chahiye. Jaise "I like reading, writing and swimming" sahi hai, kyunki teeno words **-ing** form mein hain. Par "I like reading, writing and to swim" galat hai, kyunki "to swim" ki form alag hai. Padhne wale ke dimaag ko ek jaisi form achhi lagti hai — woh sentence ko smoothly padh pata hai. Jab form badal jaati hai, toh sentence atakne lagta hai aur galat lagta hai.

Parallelism sirf verbs par nahi lagta — woh nouns, adjectives aur phrases par bhi lagta hai. "She is smart, kind and honest" sahi hai, kyunki teeno adjectives hain. "She is smart, kind and honesty" galat hai, kyunki "honesty" noun hai jabki baaki dono adjectives hain. "He came to learn, to practise and to win" sahi hai, kyunki teeno "to + verb" form mein hain. Rule simple hai: **list ke sab items ek hi family ke hone chahiye**. Agar ek item alag family ka hai, toh use badal do.

**Modifier** ka matlab hai woh word ya phrase jo kisi aur word ke baare mein extra jaankari deta hai. Jaise "the **red** car" mein "red" ek modifier hai jo car ke baare mein batata hai. Modifier hamesha us word ke **paas** hona chahiye jise woh describe karta hai. Agar modifier galat jagah par chala jaaye, toh sentence ka matlab badal jaata hai — aur yahi is article ka doosra hissa hai.`,
          `**Parallelism** means that when two or more things appear in one list inside a sentence, they must all be in the **same form**. For example, "I like reading, writing and swimming" is correct, because all three words are in the **-ing** form. But "I like reading, writing and to swim" is wrong, because "to swim" is in a different form. The reader's mind likes the same form — it can read the sentence smoothly. When the form changes, the sentence starts to stumble and feels wrong.

Parallelism applies not only to verbs — it also applies to nouns, adjectives, and phrases. "She is smart, kind and honest" is correct, because all three are adjectives. "She is smart, kind and honesty" is wrong, because "honesty" is a noun while the other two are adjectives. "He came to learn, to practise and to win" is correct, because all three are in the "to + verb" form. The rule is simple: **all items in a list must belong to the same family**. If one item belongs to a different family, change it.

A **modifier** is a word or phrase that gives extra information about another word. For example, in "the **red** car", "red" is a modifier that tells us about the car. A modifier must always sit **close to** the word it describes. If a modifier moves to the wrong place, the meaning of the sentence changes — and that is the second part of this article.`
        )
