          `Parallelism roz ki zindagi mein har jagah hai. Maan lo aap apna resume likhte ho: "Skills: reading, writing and speaking" sahi hai, kyunki teeno -ing form mein hain. Agar aap likho "Skills: reading, writing and to speak", toh padhne wala turant samajh jaayega ki kuch galat hai. Speech mein bhi parallelism kaam aata hai: "We will work hard, we will fight bravely and we will win together" mein teeno parts "we will" se shuru hote hain — isliye speech powerful lagti hai. Agar ek part ki form badal jaaye, toh speech ki rhythm toot jaati hai.

Modifiers ka real-life example dekho: "I saw a man with a telescope" ka matlab ho sakta hai ki maine telescope se aadmi ko dekha, ya maine ek aadmi ko dekha jiske paas telescope tha. Modifier "with a telescope" galat jagah par hai, isliye sentence confusing hai. Sahi sentence hai "I saw, with a telescope, a man" ya "I saw a man who had a telescope". Exam mein aise hi confusing sentences aate hain, aur aapko woh option chunna hota hai jismein modifier sahi jagah par ho.

Ek aur famous example hai **dangling modifier**: "Walking down the road, the trees looked beautiful." Yahan "walking down the road" kaun kar raha hai? Trees toh chal nahi sakte! Modifier ka subject missing hai, isliye sentence galat hai. Sahi sentence hai "Walking down the road, I saw beautiful trees." Yahan "I" walking kar raha hai, isliye modifier sahi jagah par hai.`,
          `Parallelism is everywhere in daily life. Suppose you write your resume: "Skills: reading, writing and speaking" is correct, because all three are in the -ing form. If you write "Skills: reading, writing and to speak", the reader will at once sense that something is wrong. Parallelism also matters in a speech: in "We will work hard, we will fight bravely and we will win together", all three parts start with "we will" — that is why the speech sounds powerful. If the form of one part changes, the rhythm of the speech breaks.

Look at a real-life example of modifiers: "I saw a man with a telescope" can mean that I used a telescope to see the man, or that I saw a man who had a telescope. The modifier "with a telescope" is in the wrong place, so the sentence is confusing. The correct sentence is "I saw, with a telescope, a man" or "I saw a man who had a telescope". Exams contain confusing sentences like this, and you must choose the option in which the modifier sits in the right place.

Another famous example is the **dangling modifier**: "Walking down the road, the trees looked beautiful." Who is walking down the road here? Trees cannot walk! The subject of the modifier is missing, so the sentence is wrong. The correct sentence is "Walking down the road, I saw beautiful trees." Here "I" is the one walking, so the modifier is in the right place.`
