          `Ab hum apni reading speed ko step-by-step nikaalte hain. Step 1: ek passage chuno — maan lo passage mein **300 words** hain. Step 2: timer lagao aur passage ko normal speed se padho. Maan lo aapko **90 seconds** lage. Step 3: WPM nikaalo — formula hai WPM = words x 60 / seconds. Yahan WPM = 300 x 60 / 90 = **200 WPM**. Step 4: samajh check karo — passage ke baare mein 3 sawaalon ke jawab do. Agar teeno sahi hain, toh aapki samajh achhi hai. Agar ek bhi galat hai, toh speed badhane se pehle samajh par kaam karo.

Doosra worked example: 1200 words ka passage, 200 WPM ki speed. Time kitna lagega? Time = words / speed = 1200 / 200 = **6 minutes**. Agar speed 300 WPM ho jaaye, toh time = 1200 / 300 = **4 minutes** — 2 minute bach gaye. Teesra worked example: aapne 450 words 2 minute mein padhe. WPM = 450 / 2 = **225 WPM**. Yeh average se thoda upar hai — achhi speed hai. Neeche wala code pehle example ki WPM nikaal kar print karta hai — isko run karo aur output dekho.`,
          `Now let us work out our reading speed step by step. Step 1: choose a passage — suppose the passage has **300 words**. Step 2: set a timer and read the passage at normal speed. Suppose it takes you **90 seconds**. Step 3: work out the WPM — the formula is WPM = words x 60 / seconds. Here WPM = 300 x 60 / 90 = **200 WPM**. Step 4: check your understanding — answer 3 questions about the passage. If all three are correct, your understanding is good. If even one is wrong, work on understanding before you increase speed.

Second worked example: a passage of 1200 words at a speed of 200 WPM. How much time will it take? Time = words / speed = 1200 / 200 = **6 minutes**. If the speed becomes 300 WPM, the time is 1200 / 300 = **4 minutes** — 2 minutes are saved. Third worked example: you read 450 words in 2 minutes. WPM = 450 / 2 = **225 WPM**. That is a little above average — a good speed. The code below works out the WPM of the first example and prints it — run it and look at the output.`
